Every list and prune method forwards $opts{filters} straight into params.
_request JSON-encodes a hashref value, so the encoding is right - but the
*shape* is not checked, and the shape is the thing clients get wrong.

The daemon wants a map of string to array of string:

  filters => { dangling => ["true"] }        correct
  filters => { dangling => "true" }          wrong
  filters => { dangling => 1 }               wrong
  filters => { dangling => [1] }             wrong - must be the string

A wrong shape does not error. The daemon either ignores the filter or
matches nothing, so the caller gets a plausible empty list back and no
signal. The booleans are the sharp edge: everywhere else in this client a
boolean is normalised to 1/0, and here it has to be the string "true".

Cheapest useful fix: normalise in one place - wrap a bare scalar into an
arrayref, stringify a boolean to "true"/"false" - and croak on a value that
is neither. Document it once on the shared helper rather than in each of the
six methods that take filters.

--- Concrete instance found while implementing /plugins (karr #28), 2026-08-27 ---

The plugin list filter is a case where a wrong filter name is NOT the usual silent no-match: it is a hard error, and the published Engine API reference is the thing that gets it wrong.

The reference documents the filters for GET /plugins as `capability=<name>` and `enable=<true>|<false>`. The daemon's accepted set is:

  var acceptedPluginFilterTags = map[string]bool{
    "enabled":    true,
    "capability": true,
  }

and List() runs pluginFilters.Validate(acceptedPluginFilterTags) before doing anything, so `enable` is rejected outright rather than matching nothing. A client that copies the published reference gets a failed request.

So the trap has two shapes, not one: a wrong filter *value* shape (JSON booleans instead of arrays of strings) is silent, while a wrong filter *name* is loud on endpoints that validate names -- and which endpoints validate is not documented. Worth folding into whatever validation this ticket lands on.

API::Docker::API::Plugins::list documents the correct spelling in its POD and t/plugins.t pins it.

